hadiths[13]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1915","Sayyiduna ‘Adi bin Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I asked the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) about the game that dies after being struck by the shaft of an arrow. He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Eat that (animal) which is struck by the sharp edge of the arrow, and if it is struck by the broad side, it is like being beaten with sticks.” And I also asked about the game hunted by a dog. He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “(If the dog does not eat from it itself) and keeps it for you, then eat it (because) the catching of such a dog is like slaughtering. And if, along with your dog or dogs, you see another person’s dog as well, and you suspect that your dog, together with the other, has caught and killed the game, then do not eat it, because you recited ‘Bismillah’ when releasing your own dog, not for the other dogs.”"
"1916","Sayyiduna Abu Tha’labah al-Khushani (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I asked, “O Messenger of Allah! We live in the land of the People of the Book, so can we eat from their utensils (or not)? And we live in the hunting wilderness, so can we hunt with bows and arrows, or with trained dogs, and with untrained dogs, or not? Please tell us what is correct.” He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “As for what you mentioned about the People of the Book, (the answer is) if you find utensils other than theirs, then do not eat in their utensils, and if you do not find (other utensils), then wash them well and eat in them. And (regarding hunting), if you hunt with an arrow, then recite ‘Bismillah’ and eat it. And if you send a trained dog, reciting ‘Bismillah’, and it hunts, then eating it is permissible. And if (you did not recite ‘Bismillah’ and) the dog was not trained, then after slaughtering (the animal), eating it is permissible.”"
"1917","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mughaffal (may Allah be pleased with him) that he saw a person throwing pebbles with two fingers, so he said to him, ""Do not throw like this, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade this or considered it disliked (the narrator is uncertain), and he said that there is no benefit in it: neither is any game hunted nor is any enemy wounded, but (the pebble) may break someone's tooth or put out an eye (i.e., there is no benefit except harm)."" After that, he saw him doing the same again and said, ""I narrated to you the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade or disliked throwing pebbles in this way, yet you are still doing it. Now I will not speak to you for such and such a period."""
"1918","Narrated by Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever keeps a dog that is neither for guarding livestock nor for hunting, then two Qirats from his good deeds will be reduced every day."""
"1919","The hadith of Sayyiduna ‘Adi bin Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him) (Hadith, Book: Statement regarding Slaughter and Hunting... Chapter: Regarding Saying Bismillah on the Hunt...) has passed, and in this narration, there is this addition: ...and if you shot the game with an arrow and (after being wounded) you find it dead after two or three days, and if you do not notice any sign of its death except the wound from your arrow, then eating it is permissible. But if you find it fallen in water, then eating it is not permissible."
"1920","Sayyiduna Ibn Abi Awfa (may Allah be pleased with him) says that we participated in seven or six (the narrator is unsure) battles along with the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we used to eat locusts with him."
"1921","Lady Asma bint Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with her) says: We slaughtered a horse during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and ate it, and at that time we were in Medina."
"1922","Narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) that he passed by some young men who had tied a hen and were shooting at it. When they saw Sayyiduna Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), they all ran away. Then Sayyiduna Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) said, ""Who has tied it?"" Indeed, the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the one who does such a thing."
"1923","Sayyiduna Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) in another narration says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the person who mutilates an animal."
"1924","`Sayyiduna Abu Musa Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) says that` I saw the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eating chicken."
"1925","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Tha’labah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade eating the flesh of every predatory animal that tears with its fangs."
"1926","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The example of a good friend and a bad friend is like that of a perfume seller and a blacksmith who blows the bellows. The perfume seller will either give you some perfume, or you will buy it from him, or at least you will smell a pleasant fragrance (thus, in the company of the righteous, there is benefit in every way). As for the blacksmith who blows the bellows, he will either burn your clothes (with flying sparks), or at the very least you will smell a foul odor."""
"1927","`Sayyiduna Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) narrates that` the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade striking on the face."
